From f8f96704027bef7645c4574245f9cc757a4ff0c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:06:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 220/269] gallium/pvr: build the PBE words the fragment stream
 needs, and trace what the kernel kicks

Kernel-side tracing (temporary prints in the out-of-tree powervr module, reverted after use; logs
archived in open-driver-perf/captures/real-runs/) settled the previous round's open question: the
kernel runs and kicks every job this driver submits, including the combined geom+frag kick, and the
parsed geometry command is dword-identical to the working Vulkan driver's. So the stall is on the
firmware side of the kick, and the fragment command is where ours differs.

That difference had a concrete cause: the probe built the fragment firmware stream over the same
buffer that held its PBE words, so the stream header replaced the PBE register data and the fragment
job described no render target (parsed command mostly zeros vs the reference's frame state). The
reference builds three 64-bit PBE register words per attachment (STATE_WORD0/1, REG_WORD0/1), so
pvr_csb_pbe_words() now does the same: target address/format from the pipe format, linear layout,
encoded stride, channel swizzles, pack mode. The probe builds the words first and passes them to the
stream builder.

Also marked the context-state buffers and the SR buffer the way the reference does: state buffers
bypass the device cache (GPU_UNCACHED|CPU_ACCESS) and the SR buffer is cache-line aligned - a cached
context-save buffer can hand the firmware stale state across a switch.

Verification: probe exits 0 ALL PASS under the noop shim. On the real driver the kicks still do not
complete; the remaining delta is inside the kick (context-switch task/program words and HWRT data the
firmware reads), which the archived kernel traces and reference dumps now make diffable.
